<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorothea of Montau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1347">1347</a>–<a href="/years/1394">1394</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Saint Dorothea of Montau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorothea of Montau was a mystic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. A married laywoman from Montau on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, after her husband's death she became an anchoress walled into a cell at the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> in 1393, where she died a year later. Her confessor recorded her visions, her cult flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s state, and she was canonised in 1976.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorothea-of-montau.jpg" alt="Dorothea of Montau" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcin n · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cela_bl_Doroty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dorothea was born on 6 February 1347 at Groß Montau (now Mątowy Wielkie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>), west of Marienburg, the daughter of Willem Swarte, a wealthy farmer from Holland. At 17 she was married to Adalbrecht, a swordsmith of Danzig in his forties and an ill-tempered man. Her visions began almost as soon as she was married. Her husband had little patience with them and abused her, but through her humility and gentleness she converted him, and together they went on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsiedeln-abbey">Einsiedeln Abbey</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p>Of their nine children, four died in infancy and four in the plague of 1383; the surviving daughter, Gertrude, became a Benedictine nun. Adalbrecht died in 1389 or 1390 while Dorothea, with his permission, was on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the summer of 1391 Dorothea moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> (now Kwidzyn). On 2 May 1393, with the permission of the cathedral chapter and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, she took up an anchoress's cell built against the cathedral wall, where she spent the rest of her life. She lived austerely, and many visitors came to her for advice and consolation. A devotee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passion-of-jesus">Passion of Jesus</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, she is said to have borne the stigmata.</p><p>Her confessor, the deacon Johannes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a>, a learned theologian, wrote down what she communicated to him. He composed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> life of her in seven books, the Septililium, as well as a German life in four books. The scholar Michelle Sauer has noted that her life bore a close resemblance to that of Saint Jutta of Kulmsee, a Prussian anchoress of the late 13th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dorothea died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> on 25 June 1394, the day later kept as her feast. From the moment of her death she was venerated as the guardian of the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> and patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, and in 1405 some 257 witnesses testified to her virtues and miracles. The formal process for her canonisation was then broken off and not resumed until 1955. Her relics were lost, probably during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>. Günter Grass made her life, seen through the eyes of her embittered husband, one of the subjects of his 1977 novel The Flounder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dorothea of Montau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marienwerder became the centre of the cult of <b>Dorothea of Montau</b>, a local mystic.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-pomesania">Bishopric of Pomesania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mątowy Wielkie, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Kwidzyn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.01,18.85&amp;year=1377" aria-label="Mątowy Wielkie on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Dorothea of Montau"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4443 5464h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4443 5464h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.01,18.85&amp;year=1377">What was here in 1377? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
